sal column and the single-effect arms ($L = 1$). At $L = 1$\nthere are no other effects to fold in, so gIBSS-Q2 and CAVI-Q2 run the same update; any\ndifference between arms is in the single-effect regression itself. Note the declaration\nrate at $T = 8$: a lone causal clears $\\log \\text{BF} \\ge 2$ in only about one fit in eight\n(the SER log BF pays $\\log p \\approx 5.5$ nats against an expected LRT of 8), so the $T = 8$\ncoverage rests on 25 to 30 declared sets per cell and its intervals are wide. In the $L = 5$\ngrid the three correlated causals pooled their signal and declared at over one set per fit.\n\n::: {#status-ser .cell execution_count=9}\n022-laplace-ser: 12 signal cells x 5 arms, 200-200 reps per cell; 6 null cells.\n:::\n\n\n## Headline: pooled over θ {#sec-ser-pooled}\n\n::: {#tab-ser-pooled .cell execution_count=10}\nAs the $L = 5$ headline table: CAVI-Q2 absolute, other arms as arm minus CAVI-Q2 on the same replicates, 95% CIs resample replicates. *power* is the fraction of fits whose declared set contains the causal.\n\n| T | gap | arm | coverage [95% CI] | size (IQR) | power [95% CI] | CS/fit |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 8 | - | CAVI-Q2 | 0.890 [0.835, 0.938] | 10 (5-20) | 0.107 [0.090, 0.125] | 0.12 |\n| 8 | - | gIBSS-Q2 | +0.003 [-0.006, +0.019] | 11 (5-19) | -0.003 [-0.007, -0.001] | 0.12 |\n| 8 | - | gIBSS-Laplace | +0.015 [-0.005, +0.042] | 11 (5-22) | -0.004 [-0.009, +0.000] | 0.11 |\n| 8 | - | global-JJ | -0.096 [-0.154, -0.042] | 4 (2-9) | +0.005 [-0.008, +0.018] | 0.14 |\n| 8 | - | score | -0.105 [-0.157, -0.055] | 5 (3-10) | +0.114 [+0.093, +0.135] | 0.28 |\n| 16 | - | CAVI-Q2 | 0.961 [0.944, 0.976] | 5 (2-12) | 0.416 [0.388, 0.443] | 0.43 |\n| 16 | - | gIBSS-Q2 | -0.000 [-0.001, -0.000] | 5 (2-12) | -0.004 [-0.008, -0.001] | 0.43 |\n| 16 | - | gIBSS-Laplace | +0.001 [-0.001, +0.006] | 5 (2-12) | -0.008 [-0.013, -0.003] | 0.42 |\n| 16 | - | global-JJ | -0.056 [-0.076, -0.036] | 2 (1-5) | +0.007 [-0.009, +0.023] | 0.47 |\n| 16 